{"kind":"artifact","text":"Documentation companion at exact clean head 6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4 on request/serve-drop-acknowledge-flag, cut from main 0ec3618f.\n\nTHE PART THAT MATTERS HERE IS NOT THE DELETION. Before this head, the string \"trusted processes only\" appeared nowhere under docs/ — I checked at the base commit, and its only occurrence in the whole tree was the constant at internal/service/server.go:39. The prose disclosure of the resident's trust boundary lived only inside the flag's own description and in the pages explaining why the flag was required. Deleting those passages without replacing them would have removed the boundary statement from the documentation entirely, which is the opposite of what the instruction intends. So the three pages that carried the explanation now state the boundary directly.\n\ndocs/reference/gs/serve.md, under Loopback only, now says the service is a trusted local custodian holding several actors' signing keys, that its posture is trusted processes only — every process inside this resident boundary can act as every actor key this application can open — that starting the service is the decision to accept that boundary, that it prints the same sentence beside its address on every successful start, and that loopback binding limits who can reach the service but is not a shared-host authentication system and does not separate the actors from one another inside the boundary.\n\ndocs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md carries the same posture sentence under Loopback only, and why, and keeps the sharper half explicit: loopback limits who can reach the service from outside the host, does nothing about a process already inside the boundary, and does not separate the actors there.\n\ndocs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md now opens with the boundary rather than with the flag, and states the consequence in the plainest terms: the credential names one client's session so the resident can renew and revoke it, it is not authentication between agents, and it does not protect the keys from another process running under the same account — anything running as that account can ask the resident to act as any actor whose key the repository holds. Its closing cross-reference no longer promises a required flag; it points at the deployment guide for the invocation, the readiness check, and the trust boundary you take on by running one.\n\nI judge the disclosure stronger after this change than before it. Previously a reader met the boundary as the justification for a flag, which invited reading the flag as the protection. Now the boundary is stated as a property of running the service, and the tool prints the same sentence at every start.\n\nTHE MECHANICAL HALF. docs/reference/gs/serve.md drops the flag's row from the options table and drops \"The acknowledgement is absent\" from the refusals table, and every documented invocation across serve.md, deploy-a-resident.md, configure-an-agent.md, mcp/presence.md and mcp/say.md drops the argument.\n\nONE CHANGE OUTSIDE THE REQUEST'S LISTED SCOPE, named rather than buried. docs/reference/architecture.md:181 asserted that gs serve \"requires an explicit per-invocation acknowledgement\". The removal makes that false, and AGENTS.md requires the architecture page to match the head, so the clause now reads that gs serve discloses that posture on every start. The rest of the paragraph is unchanged, including the sentence that direct local gs key access and malicious same-account processes remain outside the resident's protection. No layer contract moved; this is a factual correction the head forces.\n\nGates at this exact head, after go clean -testcache: make docs ok in 55.9s, which is the documentation citation gate and the one that governs this artifact; make vet, make build, make test and make race all exit 0 across 28 packages with zero failures and zero races; gofmt -l . empty; git status --porcelain empty. grep for the flag name returns nothing anywhere in the worktree.\n\nThis artifact covers docs. Companion artifacts at cmd/gs and README.md stand at this same head and must be co-signed with it.","body":{"commit":"6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4","path":"docs"}}